00:03:26,640 --> 00:03:28,600
So what's the central idea to an MDP?

63
00:03:28,600 --> 00:03:31,320
Well, we've really seen this in the last class, but it's such an important concept,

64
00:03:31,320 --> 00:03:36,440
I'm just going to flash up this slide again, which is the markup property.

65
00:03:36,440 --> 00:03:39,320
And again, the markup property, the central idea which we're trying to understand here

66
00:03:39,320 --> 00:03:44,400
is this idea that you have some state, this random variable s, this is characterizing where

67
00:03:44,400 --> 00:03:47,080
we are in our environment.

68
00:03:47,080 --> 00:03:52,400
And if it has the markup property, then this basically tells us that the future is independent

69
00:03:52,400 --> 00:03:53,920
of the past given the present.

70
00:03:53,920 --> 00:04:00,040
In other words, what happens next in our environment depends only on our previous state, and not

71
00:04:00,040 --> 00:04:01,920
on all the things which came before that.

72
00:04:01,920 --> 00:04:06,720
Okay, so the state completely characterizes everything which we need to know, it captures

73
00:04:06,720 --> 00:04:09,960
all the relevant information from the history.

74
00:04:09,960 --> 00:04:14,040
And once you know this thing, you can essentially, if you have ST, what this tells us is you

75
00:04:14,040 --> 00:04:16,520
can throw away everything which came before.

76
00:04:16,520 --> 00:04:21,640
So the set of markup processes or the set of states that have this property, it's the

77
00:04:21,640 --> 00:04:26,960
set of environments in which everything which we know about the system can be described

78
00:04:26,960 --> 00:04:31,840
by this single state at the current time, and we don't need to retain everything that

79
00:04:31,840 --> 00:04:36,400
we've ever seen, okay?

80
00:04:36,400 --> 00:04:42,160
So one way to understand this is that for any problem with the markup property, for any

81
00:04:42,160 --> 00:04:47,840
markup process, if you start in some state S, and you've got some successor state, S

82
00:04:47,840 --> 00:04:52,240
prime, then you can actually define the probability that you'll transition from one of those states

83
00:04:52,240 --> 00:04:53,680
to the next state.

84
00:04:53,680 --> 00:04:57,840
So remember that this state which we are in characterizes everything about what will happen

85
00:04:57,840 --> 00:04:58,840
next.

86
00:04:58,840 --> 00:05:02,560
So that must mean that there's some well-defined transition probability that tells me that

87
00:05:02,560 --> 00:05:06,320
if I was in this state before, there's some probability, given that I was in that state,

88
00:05:06,320 --> 00:05:08,640
that I will transition to some next state.

89
00:05:09,000 --> 00:05:12,600
There's some probability that if my robot is here, and I give it a little push, then

90
00:05:12,600 --> 00:05:15,880
it will fall over to here, or maybe put a foot forward.

91
00:05:15,880 --> 00:05:20,880
All of these things are completely characterized by the state it was in before, okay?

92
00:05:20,880 --> 00:05:26,080
So this gives us our state transition probabilities, so it's the probability that if I'm in S,

93
00:05:26,080 --> 00:05:30,800
I transition to S prime, and that gives us the probability that this random next state

94
00:05:30,800 --> 00:05:35,280
will be this particular instantiation of the state S prime, given that the previous state

95
00:05:35,280 --> 00:05:39,320
we were in, our current state of time, T, was this S.

96
00:05:39,320 --> 00:05:44,800
Now once we have this idea of the transition probability matrix, we can form this into

97
00:05:44,800 --> 00:05:49,480
a complete matrix that tells us, essentially, each row of this matrix tells us what would

98
00:05:49,480 --> 00:05:52,280
happen for each state that I was in.

99
00:05:52,280 --> 00:05:56,160
So if I start in state one, this tells me the probability that I'll end up in state

100
00:05:56,160 --> 00:06:00,640
one here, state two here, state three here, all the way up to N states here.

101
00:06:00,640 --> 00:06:04,160
So each row of this completely characterizes the transitions from one possible starting

102
00:06:04,160 --> 00:06:06,960
place in this Markov process.

103
00:06:06,960 --> 00:06:11,400
So this single matrix now gives me the complete structure to this Markov problem, tells me

104
00:06:11,400 --> 00:06:15,480
from any state how likely am I to end up in any other state.

105
00:06:15,480 --> 00:06:19,600
So you can imagine now that we can follow this through multiple steps and keep sampling

106
00:06:19,600 --> 00:06:26,040
from this transition probabilities, and that will give us some draws from this Markov process.

107
00:06:26,040 --> 00:06:28,640
So let's try and understand that a little bit more.

108
00:06:28,640 --> 00:06:33,120
So now just formally, a Markov process is basically a random process, a process that

109
00:06:33,120 --> 00:06:36,240
we're sort of sampling from iteratively.

110
00:06:36,240 --> 00:06:40,040
So that basically, our definition for this is going to be it's a sequence of random states.

111
00:06:40,040 --> 00:06:47,560
So a sequence of random states, S1, S2, going on in time, that has the Markov property.

112
00:06:47,560 --> 00:06:51,560
That's the definition of a Markov process, it's just a sequence of states with a Markov

113
00:06:51,560 --> 00:06:52,560
property.

114
00:06:52,560 --> 00:06:59,080
And so all it requires to define this thing is a state space, S, this is the set of states

115
00:06:59,080 --> 00:07:03,800
which we can be in, and our transition probabilities that characterize how we transition from one

116
00:07:03,800 --> 00:07:05,920
state to the next.

117
00:07:05,920 --> 00:07:09,560
And now this fully defines the dynamics of this whole system, there's some evolution

118
00:07:09,560 --> 00:07:13,920
of our robot through this system, or of our trading agent through this environment, or

119
00:07:13,920 --> 00:07:16,280
of our chess playing agent through the rules of the game.

120
00:07:16,280 --> 00:07:21,560
All of these things can be defined, no actions yet, no rewards yet, but the dynamics can

121
00:07:21,560 --> 00:07:25,120
be fully defined by a state space and a transition probability matrix.

122
00:07:25,120 --> 00:07:28,160
Okay, that's the Markov process.

