So if we take any given state here, so now if we consider class 2 state here, we know

446
00:27:47,440 --> 00:27:52,240
that no matter what we do afterwards, we're going to get the reward of minus 2 at this

447
00:27:52,240 --> 00:27:53,240
time step.

448
00:27:53,240 --> 00:27:56,440
It doesn't matter that we're going to get these other things and this plus 10 later,

449
00:27:56,440 --> 00:27:59,400
that's irrelevant because we're just looking at how much reward we get at this time step

450
00:27:59,400 --> 00:28:00,400
onwards.

451
00:28:00,400 --> 00:28:02,760
So the value of being in this state is minus 2.

452
00:28:03,760 --> 00:28:04,760
That's what we're going to get.

453
00:28:04,760 --> 00:28:07,760
We're going to get minus 2 whether we go to sleep or whether we go this way, we're going

454
00:28:07,760 --> 00:28:08,760
to get minus 2.

455
00:28:08,760 --> 00:28:10,760
That's just the definition of that.

456
00:28:10,760 --> 00:28:17,320
However, if we look at a more long-sighted discount factor, so this is at 0.9 now, then

457
00:28:17,320 --> 00:28:22,040
the values all change and now we have to consider, well, if I was in this state, I'm going to

458
00:28:22,040 --> 00:28:26,560
get a reward of minus 2 now, but then with 0.8 probability, I'm going to transition

459
00:28:26,560 --> 00:28:31,400
over here into a situation where I'm going to get additional rewards minus 2 and then

460
00:28:31,440 --> 00:28:36,600
plus 10 again and so forth, and when we factor that all together on computer and we'll see

461
00:28:36,600 --> 00:28:41,880
how to compute these things later as we go on, the value of this state actually becomes

462
00:28:41,880 --> 00:28:42,880
much better.

463
00:28:42,880 --> 00:28:47,320
We can get 0.9 reward from this state onwards, averaging over the probability of falling

464
00:28:47,320 --> 00:28:51,120
asleep and continuing through to classes.

465
00:28:51,120 --> 00:28:52,120
So that's the value.

466
00:28:52,120 --> 00:28:53,120
That's the average.

467
00:28:53,120 --> 00:28:56,520
Now, the expectation over all of the different paths that we can take through this system

468
00:28:56,520 --> 00:29:00,360
and take an average of them, that's the number that summarizes how good it is to be in that

469
00:29:00,360 --> 00:29:01,360
state.

470
00:29:01,360 --> 00:29:04,040
So that's 0.9 units of reward from then onwards if we start there.

471
00:29:04,040 --> 00:29:11,680
If I drop you into that class, 0.9, that's how good you are.

472
00:29:11,680 --> 00:29:16,800
So now we're going to talk about maybe the most fundamental relationship in reinforcement

473
00:29:16,800 --> 00:29:17,800
learning.

474
00:29:17,800 --> 00:29:23,160
I might have come across this in dynamic programming, it's very widely used, and it's called the

475
00:29:23,160 --> 00:29:24,160
Bellman equation.

476
00:29:24,160 --> 00:29:30,200
And the idea is that the value function sort of obeys this recursive decomposition, and

477
00:29:30,200 --> 00:29:35,080
the main idea is very simple, that it basically says that you can take your sequence of rewards

478
00:29:35,080 --> 00:29:39,640
from this time step all the way onwards, and you can break it up into two parts, which

479
00:29:39,640 --> 00:29:43,920
basically consists of the immediate reward that you're going to get, and then the value

480
00:29:43,920 --> 00:29:46,320
that you'll get from that time step onwards.

481
00:29:46,320 --> 00:29:49,280
So you break it up into immediate reward, and then from the successor state that you

482
00:29:49,280 --> 00:29:54,040
end up in, the value of where you end up, so immediate reward plus the value of where

483
00:29:54,040 --> 00:29:55,040
you end up.

484
00:29:55,040 --> 00:30:00,400
Now if I'm a robot, I might get an immediate reward of like plus 10, but then I end up

485
00:30:00,400 --> 00:30:03,120
in this new state, and the question is how good is it to be in this new state?

486
00:30:03,120 --> 00:30:07,560
And so the overall value function of being here is like my 10 points of immediate reward

487
00:30:07,560 --> 00:30:11,640
plus the value of where I ended up, and that's what the Bellman equation tells me.

488
00:30:11,640 --> 00:30:14,920
So formally if we just step through this, it's very straightforward, so this is our

489
00:30:14,920 --> 00:30:15,920
value function.

490
00:30:15,920 --> 00:30:19,960
The value function is the expected return, given that I start in a particular state.

491
00:30:19,960 --> 00:30:23,080
And that's how we want to know how good is it to be in this state, and it's the expected

492
00:30:23,120 --> 00:30:25,280
return if you start in that state.

493
00:30:25,280 --> 00:30:27,760
We just unwrap the definition of return.

494
00:30:27,760 --> 00:30:32,440
The return is just the sum of these rewards going into the future, discounted.

495
00:30:32,440 --> 00:30:38,640
We can break down those sum of rewards into the immediate reward plus the discounted value,

496
00:30:38,640 --> 00:30:41,080
the discounted return from the next step.

497
00:30:41,080 --> 00:30:44,720
So we've got a discount, we just pull out the discount factor we've got, starting from

498
00:30:44,720 --> 00:30:50,200
RT plus 2 plus gamma RT plus 3, and so forth, okay, just pull the factor of gamma out.

499
00:30:50,200 --> 00:30:55,040
And now we see that this thing here is just the return from the next time step, the return

500
00:30:55,040 --> 00:30:57,560
starting from T plus 2 instead of from T plus 1.

501
00:30:57,560 --> 00:31:02,360
So that's this thing here, we say it's the expected immediate reward plus discounted

502
00:31:02,360 --> 00:31:03,360
