702
00:38:47,720 --> 00:38:48,680
Then I'll move on.

703
00:38:48,680 --> 00:38:52,320
Is there a reason why one needs to exit the past state

704
00:38:52,320 --> 00:38:55,440
to get the reward of what's done in the future?

705
00:38:55,440 --> 00:38:57,440
Yes.

706
00:38:57,440 --> 00:38:59,360
Yes, thank you.

707
00:38:59,360 --> 00:39:02,280
OK, let's move on.

708
00:39:02,280 --> 00:39:04,960
OK, so this Bellman equation, so far we've

709
00:39:04,960 --> 00:39:08,280
seen how to spell this out literally state by state.

710
00:39:08,280 --> 00:39:10,040
But there's a much more concise formulation,

711
00:39:10,040 --> 00:39:13,400
which is using matrices and vectors.

712
00:39:13,400 --> 00:39:16,760
So if we just spend a moment to understand that, what we can do

713
00:39:16,760 --> 00:39:19,760
is basically use this matrix formulation of the transition

714
00:39:19,760 --> 00:39:21,320
matrix, which we've already seen.

715
00:39:21,320 --> 00:39:22,520
But now what we're going to do is

716
00:39:22,520 --> 00:39:25,240
we're going to introduce a vector representation

717
00:39:25,240 --> 00:39:28,000
of the value function, which is the obvious thing.

718
00:39:28,000 --> 00:39:30,800
It's basically we form a column vector,

719
00:39:30,800 --> 00:39:33,160
where each element of the column vector

720
00:39:33,160 --> 00:39:37,400
contains the value function of a specific state for all states

721
00:39:37,400 --> 00:39:39,520
from 1 to n.

722
00:39:39,520 --> 00:39:41,920
And so now we can write out our Bellman equation very

723
00:39:41,920 --> 00:39:42,480
concisely.

724
00:39:42,480 --> 00:39:44,800
It's just that the value function that you start with,

725
00:39:44,800 --> 00:39:46,960
this column vector here, is equal to,

726
00:39:46,960 --> 00:39:49,520
and we're going to do the same thing with the reward function

727
00:39:49,520 --> 00:39:49,800
as well.

728
00:39:49,800 --> 00:39:52,120
We're going to write out the reward function with one

729
00:39:52,120 --> 00:39:53,360
element per state.

730
00:39:53,360 --> 00:39:56,560
This tells us how much reward you'll get from exiting state 1,

731
00:39:56,560 --> 00:39:58,880
all the way down to how much reward you'll get from exiting state

732
00:39:58,880 --> 00:40:01,080
n, so forth.

733
00:40:01,080 --> 00:40:03,000
So this is your immediate reward.

734
00:40:03,000 --> 00:40:06,280
And then this is how much, this is where you'll transition to.

735
00:40:06,280 --> 00:40:10,120
And this is how much value you started with before.

736
00:40:10,120 --> 00:40:13,760
So it's basically telling us that in one sweep,

737
00:40:13,800 --> 00:40:17,280
we can just look at this thing and say the value of their state

738
00:40:17,280 --> 00:40:22,880
is equal to the immediate reward plus the transition matrix.

739
00:40:22,880 --> 00:40:25,200
So these are all the places we might transition to.

740
00:40:25,200 --> 00:40:27,480
And then this is the value of where we end up.

741
00:40:27,480 --> 00:40:29,520
This is the value of after I take this step,

742
00:40:29,520 --> 00:40:31,520
I might end up in this state, I might end up in this state,

743
00:40:31,520 --> 00:40:32,920
I might end up in this state.

744
00:40:32,920 --> 00:40:35,160
And each row of this thing is going

745
00:40:35,160 --> 00:40:37,120
to be dot-productive with these values.

746
00:40:37,120 --> 00:40:40,200
There's some probability if I'm in this state

747
00:40:40,200 --> 00:40:42,400
that I end up in each of these successor states.

748
00:40:42,400 --> 00:40:44,040
And then we take the dot product of that

749
00:40:44,040 --> 00:40:45,920
with all of the values of where we end up.

750
00:40:45,920 --> 00:40:48,360
And that's going to give us this first entry here.

751
00:40:48,360 --> 00:40:50,120
So it gives us, literally, it's a way

752
00:40:50,120 --> 00:40:52,320
of writing out the entire Bellman equation in one

753
00:40:52,320 --> 00:40:54,560
very concise form.

754
00:40:54,560 --> 00:40:55,120
OK.

755
00:40:55,120 --> 00:40:56,120
Yeah, question?

756
00:40:56,120 --> 00:41:00,520
The Bellman equation is number one.

757
00:41:00,520 --> 00:41:02,680
Yes, that should be pn1, actually, from the bottom left.

758
00:41:06,520 --> 00:41:07,200
OK.

759
00:41:07,200 --> 00:41:10,720
So the Bellman equation is a linear equation.

760
00:41:10,760 --> 00:41:13,240
As a result, it can be solved directly.

761
00:41:13,240 --> 00:41:15,240
It's one of the nice things about linear equations.

762
00:41:15,240 --> 00:41:18,720
This is not going to be true once we move to the more complex case

763
00:41:18,720 --> 00:41:20,440
with Markov precision processes.

764
00:41:20,440 --> 00:41:22,960
It's a nice property of just evaluating the rewards.

765
00:41:22,960 --> 00:41:24,600
But once we want to maximize rewards,

766
00:41:24,600 --> 00:41:26,040
it's super gets harder.

767
00:41:26,040 --> 00:41:28,560
But at this stage, at least, we can solve this directly.

768
00:41:28,560 --> 00:41:31,720
Assuming that our matrix is small enough to invert,

769
00:41:31,720 --> 00:41:34,840
what we can do is just write out our linear equation now.

770
00:41:34,840 --> 00:41:37,560
All we're going to do is collect together the v terms here.

771
00:41:37,600 --> 00:41:43,560
So we've got identity times v here and gamma p times v here.

772
00:41:43,560 --> 00:41:47,200
So we collect those together on this side, i minus gamma p,

773
00:41:47,200 --> 00:41:50,720
v equals r, and then we just invert this matrix.

774
00:41:50,720 --> 00:41:52,720
OK.

775
00:41:52,720 --> 00:41:59,400
And this is the solution to the MDP.

776
00:41:59,400 --> 00:42:02,120
The computational complexity of inverting this matrix

777
00:42:02,120 --> 00:42:04,880
is n cubed for n states.

778
00:42:04,880 --> 00:42:07,600
So this is not typically a practical solution method

779
00:42:07,600 --> 00:42:10,040
for large Markov processes.

780
00:42:10,040 --> 00:42:11,960
So what we'll start to look at in subsequent lectures

781
00:42:11,960 --> 00:42:14,920
is efficient ways to solve these problems.

782
00:42:14,920 --> 00:42:17,240
In particular, this is just going to be a building block.

783
00:42:17,240 --> 00:42:18,400
All of the things we've seen so far

784
00:42:18,400 --> 00:42:20,560
are just building blocks towards what we really care about,

785
00:42:20,560 --> 00:42:22,040
which is making decisions.

786
00:42:22,040 --> 00:42:23,640
And so this is just going to be a building block.

787
00:42:23,640 --> 00:42:25,360
We need to be able to efficiently figure out

788
00:42:25,360 --> 00:42:27,200
what's the value of being in this state.

789
00:42:27,200 --> 00:42:29,680
We can't always do this.

790
00:42:29,680 --> 00:42:32,520
But it is nice to know that as well, Markov reward processes,

791
00:42:32,520 --> 00:42:33,840
we can just invert this thing.

792
00:42:33,840 --> 00:42:37,520
And it gives us some intuition into what's going on.

793
00:42:37,520 --> 00:42:40,240
So later, we'll talk about the next lecture, in fact.

794
00:42:40,240 --> 00:42:42,400
The next class is dynamic programming.

795
00:42:42,400 --> 00:42:43,960
That's a very well-known class of methods

796
00:42:43,960 --> 00:42:46,720
for basically doing this more efficiently.

797
00:42:46,720 --> 00:42:48,720
Then we'll talk about Monte Carlo evaluation

798
00:42:48,720 --> 00:42:49,880
and temporal difference learning.

799
00:42:49,880 --> 00:42:51,400
That's like the next couple of lectures

800
00:42:51,400 --> 00:42:55,040
are basically doing exactly that.

801
00:42:55,040 --> 00:42:56,040
Right.

802
00:42:56,040 --> 00:42:58,800
So that was sort of the elementary building

803
00:42:58,800 --> 00:43:01,480
blocks leading up to this point, which is the MDP.

804
00:43:01,480 --> 00:43:02,920
This is the thing which we actually

805
00:43:02,920 --> 00:43:05,560
do in reinforcement learning.

806
00:43:05,560 --> 00:43:07,880
And so I guess I should pause.

807
00:43:07,880 --> 00:43:09,800
Any questions so far?

808
00:43:09,800 --> 00:43:11,480
I guess we've been doing them as we go, so.

809
00:43:15,360 --> 00:43:16,240
Right.

810
00:43:16,240 --> 00:43:19,680
So the MDP, we're basically going to add in one more piece

811
00:43:19,680 --> 00:43:21,240
of complexity now, which is actions.

812
00:43:21,240 --> 00:43:23,440
So so far, there's been nothing for us to do.

813
00:43:23,440 --> 00:43:24,680
I just told you, you get plunked down

814
00:43:24,680 --> 00:43:28,080
in your student Markov reward process.

815
00:43:28,080 --> 00:43:30,040
And you just randomly sell up all these transitions

816
00:43:30,040 --> 00:43:31,640
and get spat out at the other end.

817
00:43:31,640 --> 00:43:34,000
You didn't get any, you didn't have any agency.

818
00:43:34,000 --> 00:43:36,360
You weren't able to take actions and make decisions.

819
00:43:36,360 --> 00:43:37,400
So let's now change that.

820
00:43:37,400 --> 00:43:38,840
Let's introduce decisions.

821
00:43:38,840 --> 00:43:40,560
So what we're going to do now is basically

822
00:43:40,560 --> 00:43:42,560
take our Markov reward process, which

823
00:43:42,560 --> 00:43:45,560
was a state space transition matrix, reward function,

824
00:43:45,560 --> 00:43:48,040
and discount factor, and add in one more component, which

825
00:43:48,040 --> 00:43:51,080
is the action space.

826
00:43:51,080 --> 00:43:53,880
So A is now a finite set of actions.

827
00:43:53,880 --> 00:43:55,280
In the extensions to the class, you'll

828
00:43:55,280 --> 00:44:00,160
see how this can be made continuous or infinite.

829
00:44:00,160 --> 00:44:02,880
But now we'll consider the simplest case.

830
00:44:02,880 --> 00:44:08,120
This is a discrete set of actions, finite size.

831
00:44:08,120 --> 00:44:09,560
And so now what we're going to do is

832
00:44:09,560 --> 00:44:12,040
say the transition probability matrix now

833
00:44:12,040 --> 00:44:14,280
depends on which action we take.

834
00:44:14,280 --> 00:44:16,760
So where you end up is now going to depend

835
00:44:16,760 --> 00:44:18,240
on the action that you take.

836
00:44:18,240 --> 00:44:20,280
You take, if you move to the left,

837
00:44:20,280 --> 00:44:22,440
the probability that you end up in all these different states

838
00:44:22,440 --> 00:44:25,120
is going to be different if you choose to move to the right.

839
00:44:25,120 --> 00:44:27,880
The wind might blow you different ways in each case,

840
00:44:27,920 --> 00:44:30,640
but you have some agency over where you're going to end up.

841
00:44:30,640 --> 00:44:32,640
So the way we think of this is that there's just one,

842
00:44:32,640 --> 00:44:35,760
in the discrete case, there's like one separate matrix

843
00:44:35,760 --> 00:44:39,840
of transition probabilities for each action that you might take.

844
00:44:39,840 --> 00:44:41,880
And now we've got this reward function.

845
00:44:41,880 --> 00:44:45,520
The reward function, again, may depend on the action.

846
00:44:45,520 --> 00:44:48,520
It may also not depend on the action, but in general,

847
00:44:48,520 --> 00:44:50,480
this thing can depend on the action.

848
00:44:50,480 --> 00:44:51,920
Apart from that, everything is the same.

849
00:44:51,920 --> 00:44:52,720
The machinery is the same.

850
00:44:52,720 --> 00:44:56,840
We've just introduced actions in the simplest possible way.

851
00:44:56,840 --> 00:44:58,840
So what we're going to do is actually

852
00:44:58,840 --> 00:45:03,200
redo our student marker process now, but as an MDP,

853
00:45:03,200 --> 00:45:05,240
where there's decisions that you can take.

854
00:45:05,240 --> 00:45:10,200
So the decisions are now these red labels on the arcs.

855
00:45:10,200 --> 00:45:12,200
And so the point now is that you actually

856
00:45:12,200 --> 00:45:13,680
have some agency here.

857
00:45:13,680 --> 00:45:16,000
And so from this state, you can choose to either study.

858
00:45:16,000 --> 00:45:19,160
That's a choice now, so there's no probabilities attached to this.

859
00:45:19,160 --> 00:45:20,760
You can choose to study.

860
00:45:20,760 --> 00:45:24,080
If you study, you end up in the state.

861
00:45:24,080 --> 00:45:27,560
If you choose to go to Facebook, you end up in this state.

862
00:45:27,560 --> 00:45:29,680
When you're at Facebook, you can choose to do it again,

863
00:45:29,680 --> 00:45:32,080
or you can choose to quit.

864
00:45:32,080 --> 00:45:34,200
You can choose to go to sleep here, or to study again.

865
00:45:34,200 --> 00:45:37,320
You can choose to study again, or you can choose to go to the pub.

866
00:45:37,320 --> 00:45:39,600
If you choose to go to the pub, that's the only place now

867
00:45:39,600 --> 00:45:41,160
in that there's any randomness.

868
00:45:41,160 --> 00:45:43,560
If you choose to go to the pub, who knows what might happen?

869
00:45:43,560 --> 00:45:46,520
Maybe you'll drink one or two beers, not quite sure.

870
00:45:46,520 --> 00:45:49,880
So now there's some probability that you'll end up going back

871
00:45:49,880 --> 00:45:52,480
to class one, going back to class two,

872
00:45:52,480 --> 00:45:56,240
or even going back to class three.

873
00:45:56,240 --> 00:45:59,600
So there's a lot more control that you can exert now

874
00:45:59,600 --> 00:46:01,960
over your path through this MDP.

875
00:46:01,960 --> 00:46:03,720
This is the reality for an agent.

876
00:46:03,720 --> 00:46:05,720
And the goal is, the game we're playing now

877
00:46:05,720 --> 00:46:10,040
is to try and find the best path through your decision-making

878
00:46:10,040 --> 00:46:12,920
process that maximizes the sum of rewards that you get.

879
00:46:12,920 --> 00:46:16,800
That's the game that you play in an MDP.

880
00:46:16,800 --> 00:46:18,760
So to do that, the first thing we need to talk about

881
00:46:18,760 --> 00:46:21,320
is formalizing what it means to make decisions,

882
00:46:21,320 --> 00:46:22,480
and to take decisions.

883
00:46:22,480 --> 00:46:25,360
And to do that, we define something called the policy.

884
00:46:25,360 --> 00:46:28,000
So the policy is basically, we're

885
00:46:28,000 --> 00:46:29,880
going to talk about stochastic policies today.

886
00:46:29,880 --> 00:46:32,880
And these policies are distributions over actions

887
00:46:32,880 --> 00:46:34,120
given states.

888
00:46:34,120 --> 00:46:37,080
So in other words, if you're in some state S,

889
00:46:37,080 --> 00:46:39,160
this distribution basically gives you the mapping.

890
00:46:39,160 --> 00:46:42,840
It says, if I'm in this state, map from that state

891
00:46:42,840 --> 00:46:44,640
to some probability of going left

892
00:46:44,640 --> 00:46:46,200
and some probability of going right.

893
00:46:46,200 --> 00:46:48,880
So this is the thing that tells me, if I'm in class 1,

894
00:46:48,880 --> 00:46:52,640
I am going to choose to go to class 2 with probability 0.9.

895
00:46:52,640 --> 00:46:54,920
It's going to Facebook with probability 0.1.

896
00:46:54,920 --> 00:46:56,520
This is something under the agent's control.

897
00:46:56,520 --> 00:46:57,800
It's something we decide.

898
00:46:57,800 --> 00:47:00,960
And it's just a stochastic transition matrix.

899
00:47:00,960 --> 00:47:02,440
And it's useful to make it stochastic,

900
00:47:02,440 --> 00:47:04,480
because that allows us to do things like exploration.

901
00:47:04,480 --> 00:47:09,320
So we'll make use of that later in the course.

902
00:47:09,320 --> 00:47:10,920
So once you have a policy, it completely

903
00:47:10,920 --> 00:47:12,480
defines how that agent will behave.

904
00:47:12,480 --> 00:47:19,800
And in an MDP, the policies depend just on the current state.

905
00:47:19,800 --> 00:47:21,440
That was the Markov property.

906
00:47:21,440 --> 00:47:23,080
Doesn't depend on the history.

907
00:47:23,080 --> 00:47:27,040
And as a result, we consider what we call stationary policies.

908
00:47:27,040 --> 00:47:32,440
We don't consider all of the policies the same no matter

909
00:47:32,440 --> 00:47:34,560
what time step we're in in the MDP.

910
00:47:34,560 --> 00:47:36,960
We just have the same policy at each time step.

911
00:47:36,960 --> 00:47:38,760
And the only thing that this depends on

912
00:47:38,760 --> 00:47:40,960
is the state that we're in, not the time step at which it

913
00:47:40,960 --> 00:47:42,040
happened.

914
00:47:42,080 --> 00:47:44,000
And that's sufficient to behave optimally,

915
00:47:44,000 --> 00:47:46,320
because by definition, we have the Markov property.

916
00:47:46,320 --> 00:47:48,480
And this state, S, fully characterizes everything

917
00:47:48,480 --> 00:47:50,000
that will happen next.

918
00:47:50,000 --> 00:47:51,000
Yeah, question?

919
00:47:51,000 --> 00:47:54,480
How do we reward the element here?

920
00:47:54,480 --> 00:47:56,920
And I'm sure you want to maximize rewards

921
00:47:56,920 --> 00:47:59,400
in terms of the fact that they're not.

922
00:47:59,400 --> 00:48:02,840
The question was, why are there no rewards in this equation?

923
00:48:02,840 --> 00:48:05,360
And that's because the state, S, fully characterizes

924
00:48:05,360 --> 00:48:06,680
your future rewards.

925
00:48:06,680 --> 00:48:10,480
So in a Markov reward process or Markov decision process,

926
00:48:10,480 --> 00:48:12,600
the Markov property means that S fully characterizes

927
00:48:12,600 --> 00:48:16,560
the evolution from this state onwards in the process.

928
00:48:16,560 --> 00:48:18,880
And so what you're looking for is the policy, which,

929
00:48:18,880 --> 00:48:21,200
given the state you're in, well, you

930
00:48:21,200 --> 00:48:23,960
want to pick actions that will get you the most future reward.

931
00:48:23,960 --> 00:48:25,880
So all you need to look at is what state am I in

932
00:48:25,880 --> 00:48:27,560
and what action should I take next?

933
00:48:27,560 --> 00:48:29,360
And we're trying to pick those actions in the way,

934
00:48:29,360 --> 00:48:32,080
given that state, that will get us the most future reward.

935
00:48:32,080 --> 00:48:33,320
So the rewards are in the future.

936
00:48:33,320 --> 00:48:35,560
We don't care about how many rewards we've got in the past.

937
00:48:35,560 --> 00:48:36,480
Those rewards have gone now.

938
00:48:36,480 --> 00:48:37,840
They've been consumed.

939
00:48:37,840 --> 00:48:39,360
All we care about is how much reward

940
00:48:39,360 --> 00:48:41,240
we can get from now into the future.

941
00:48:41,240 --> 00:48:43,880
So we don't actually care when we try to maximize this thing.

942
00:48:43,880 --> 00:48:45,960
We don't care if we currently, if we've already

943
00:48:45,960 --> 00:48:47,960
achieved a million reward or minus a million reward.

944
00:48:47,960 --> 00:48:48,600
That's irrelevant.

945
00:48:48,600 --> 00:48:51,760
What we want to do is maximize the reward from now onwards.

946
00:48:51,760 --> 00:48:53,480
That's the way we're going to behave optimally.

947
00:48:53,480 --> 00:48:56,080
I would give commission on state information-wise

948
00:48:56,080 --> 00:48:58,240
to produce all the information that I would

949
00:48:58,240 --> 00:48:59,600
expect to be the reward state.

950
00:48:59,600 --> 00:49:00,600
Yes.

951
00:49:00,600 --> 00:49:01,720
Yes, exactly.

952
00:49:04,760 --> 00:49:05,240
OK.

953
00:49:05,240 --> 00:49:07,840
So one important thing to note about the connection

954
00:49:07,840 --> 00:49:10,400
between Markov decision processes and Markov reward

955
00:49:10,400 --> 00:49:15,120
processes is that we can always recover an MRP or a Markov

956
00:49:15,120 --> 00:49:17,760
process from our decision process.

957
00:49:17,760 --> 00:49:20,120
So just to understand that, we can

958
00:49:20,120 --> 00:49:23,560
think about basically the sample of states that we draw.

959
00:49:23,560 --> 00:49:26,720
Like if we have some policy that helps us pick actions,

960
00:49:26,720 --> 00:49:29,440
we're just going to draw this sequence of states.

961
00:49:29,440 --> 00:49:31,080
And the sequence of states that we draw,

962
00:49:31,080 --> 00:49:33,120
when we follow our particular process,

963
00:49:33,120 --> 00:49:36,000
is actually a Markov chain, so a Markov process itself.

964
00:49:36,000 --> 00:49:39,080
No matter what policy we choose, that policy defines

965
00:49:39,080 --> 00:49:41,600
some Markov chain that actually defines our dynamics the way

966
00:49:41,600 --> 00:49:45,160
that we're going to move through and evolve through this system.

967
00:49:45,160 --> 00:49:47,560
And if we look at the sequence of states and rewards

968
00:49:47,560 --> 00:49:49,560
that we receive as we pass through,

969
00:49:49,560 --> 00:49:52,240
like once we fix the policy, if you fix the policy

970
00:49:52,240 --> 00:49:54,560
and look just at the sequence of states and rewards that you see

971
00:49:54,560 --> 00:49:57,480
as you go through this MDP, that sequence

972
00:49:57,480 --> 00:49:59,760
is a Markov reward process.

973
00:49:59,760 --> 00:50:01,320
And the way to understand that is

974
00:50:01,320 --> 00:50:04,160
that we're going to define basically transition dynamics

975
00:50:04,200 --> 00:50:07,560
and reward function with just average over our policy.

976
00:50:07,560 --> 00:50:09,520
We're just going to average over all the things which

977
00:50:09,520 --> 00:50:10,520
happen under our policy.

978
00:50:10,520 --> 00:50:11,800
We're going to average over.

979
00:50:11,800 --> 00:50:13,960
So we're going to define the transition dynamics

980
00:50:13,960 --> 00:50:17,360
to be the average of the transition dynamics for all

981
00:50:17,360 --> 00:50:19,040
of the things that we might do.

982
00:50:19,040 --> 00:50:21,320
So if we're going to go left with probability 0.5

983
00:50:21,320 --> 00:50:23,240
and right with probability 0.5, I'm just

984
00:50:23,240 --> 00:50:25,880
going to take 0.5 times the dynamics,

985
00:50:25,880 --> 00:50:28,200
or all the probability of going to all the states from here,

986
00:50:28,200 --> 00:50:30,240
plus 0.5 times the probability of all the states

987
00:50:30,240 --> 00:50:32,000
that I'm going to end up in over here.

988
00:50:32,000 --> 00:50:34,680
Sum those together, and those average dynamics

989
00:50:34,680 --> 00:50:38,680
define some Markov reward process.

990
00:50:38,680 --> 00:50:41,760
So we can always flatten our MDP given our current policy

991
00:50:41,760 --> 00:50:45,120
back into a Markov chain.

992
00:50:45,120 --> 00:50:46,240
This is just a useful point.

993
00:50:46,240 --> 00:50:47,240
It's not like central.

994
00:50:47,240 --> 00:50:50,000
So you can come back to this and think about it offline

995
00:50:50,000 --> 00:50:52,560
if you like.

996
00:50:52,560 --> 00:50:55,360
OK, what is central is the concept of the value function.

997
00:50:55,360 --> 00:50:56,760
So we already had the value function

998
00:50:56,760 --> 00:50:59,120
for a Markov reward process, but there was no agency there.

999
00:50:59,120 --> 00:51:00,480
There was no decisions.

1000
00:51:00,480 --> 00:51:01,560
Now we've got this policy.

1001
00:51:01,600 --> 00:51:02,720
There's some way that we can choose

1002
00:51:02,720 --> 00:51:05,040
to behave in our Markov process.

1003
00:51:05,040 --> 00:51:07,440
I might be following this path straight through the student

1004
00:51:07,440 --> 00:51:10,120
Markov chain where I collect all the rewards

1005
00:51:10,120 --> 00:51:11,200
by going through all the classes,

1006
00:51:11,200 --> 00:51:14,080
or I might choose to spend a lot of time on Facebook.

1007
00:51:14,080 --> 00:51:16,040
These are going to give different rewards.

1008
00:51:16,040 --> 00:51:17,840
There's not one expectation anymore.

1009
00:51:17,840 --> 00:51:20,640
There are different expectations depending on how I behave.

1010
00:51:20,640 --> 00:51:22,720
And so we subscript our value function

1011
00:51:22,720 --> 00:51:26,160
by the policy that we're interested in evaluating.

1012
00:51:26,160 --> 00:51:31,040
So v pi of s now tells us how good is it to be in state s

1013
00:51:31,040 --> 00:51:34,560
if I'm following policy pi?

1014
00:51:34,560 --> 00:51:36,000
So if I'm following the policy that just

1015
00:51:36,000 --> 00:51:37,440
goes straight through the classes,

1016
00:51:37,440 --> 00:51:38,920
how much reward will I get?

1017
00:51:38,920 --> 00:51:41,320
Or if I'm following the policy where I stay on Facebook

1018
00:51:41,320 --> 00:51:42,960
for as long as possible, how much reward

1019
00:51:42,960 --> 00:51:45,800
will I get from each state on this?

1020
00:51:45,800 --> 00:51:47,800
So this basically is defined.

1021
00:51:47,800 --> 00:51:49,480
We've just got this expectation here now.

1022
00:51:49,480 --> 00:51:52,960
This e pi basically means the expectation

1023
00:51:52,960 --> 00:51:56,520
when we sample all actions according to this policy pi.

1024
00:51:56,520 --> 00:51:59,960
That's sort of hidden in that e pi notation.

1025
00:52:00,000 --> 00:52:03,040
We're also going to define a second type of value function

1026
00:52:03,040 --> 00:52:05,160
called the action value function.

1027
00:52:05,160 --> 00:52:07,240
So so far we've got the state value function.

1028
00:52:07,240 --> 00:52:09,320
This v we call the state value function

1029
00:52:09,320 --> 00:52:12,560
that tells us how good is it to be in a particular state s.

1030
00:52:12,560 --> 00:52:14,880
We're also going to define the action value function

1031
00:52:14,880 --> 00:52:17,200
which tells us how good is it to take a particular action

1032
00:52:17,200 --> 00:52:18,880
from a particular state.

1033
00:52:18,880 --> 00:52:21,360
And this is the thing which we intuitively care about

1034
00:52:21,360 --> 00:52:23,560
when we want to decide which action should I take?

1035
00:52:23,560 --> 00:52:25,480
Should I go left or should I go right?

1036
00:52:25,480 --> 00:52:26,440
Well, I should choose the one that's

1037
00:52:26,440 --> 00:52:28,280
going to give me more reward.

1038
00:52:28,280 --> 00:52:30,160
And the way I evaluate these things

1039
00:52:30,160 --> 00:52:33,000
is by looking at the action values.

1040
00:52:33,000 --> 00:52:35,400
In other words, we're basically going to look at,

1041
00:52:35,400 --> 00:52:39,120
so q pi, so for a given policy, for a given way to behave,

1042
00:52:39,120 --> 00:52:41,040
we want to know if I'm in this state s

1043
00:52:41,040 --> 00:52:44,400
and I take this action a, what's the expected return,

1044
00:52:44,400 --> 00:52:47,520
the total reward that I'll get after I've taken that action,

1045
00:52:47,520 --> 00:52:50,720
how much reward will I get from that point onwards?

1046
00:52:50,720 --> 00:52:53,000
OK, that's the key quantity that we're

1047
00:52:53,000 --> 00:52:55,120
going to use to help us optimize our MDP

1048
00:52:55,120 --> 00:52:58,480
and pick the best actions.

1049
00:52:58,480 --> 00:53:00,480
So let's make this concrete again.

1050
00:53:00,480 --> 00:53:03,520
So this is the state value function for the student MDP,

1051
00:53:03,520 --> 00:53:06,720
undiscounted, so the discount factor is 1.

1052
00:53:06,720 --> 00:53:08,920
And this is just for uniform random behavior.

1053
00:53:08,920 --> 00:53:10,680
So we're fixing the policy to say,

1054
00:53:10,680 --> 00:53:12,520
we're always, when we've got a choice,

1055
00:53:12,520 --> 00:53:14,160
we're always going to randomly pick

1056
00:53:14,160 --> 00:53:18,000
50-50 between those choices.

1057
00:53:18,000 --> 00:53:21,520
And so this is the value function that we end up with.

1058
00:53:21,520 --> 00:53:24,760
We see that we end up thinking it's not very good to start

1059
00:53:24,760 --> 00:53:26,000
in this state here because there's

1060
00:53:26,000 --> 00:53:29,680
a high probability of ending up in the Facebook state

1061
00:53:29,680 --> 00:53:31,360
because we're choosing things 50-50.

1062
00:53:37,760 --> 00:53:40,320
Now what we can define is another Bellman equation.

1063
00:53:40,320 --> 00:53:43,160
So the Bellman equation, we saw this in the Markov reward

1064
00:53:43,160 --> 00:53:46,520
process, and now we're going to define a Bellman equation

1065
00:53:46,520 --> 00:53:50,720
for these value functions in the MDP case.

1066
00:53:50,720 --> 00:53:52,560
So again, we can use the same idea,

1067
00:53:52,560 --> 00:53:54,640
that the value function can be decomposed

1068
00:53:54,640 --> 00:53:57,560
into an immediate reward plus the discounted value

1069
00:53:57,560 --> 00:53:58,360
at the next state.

1070
00:53:58,360 --> 00:54:01,000
So again, there's this idea that wherever you are,

1071
00:54:01,000 --> 00:54:04,200
you take one step, and you get your immediate reward

1072
00:54:04,200 --> 00:54:06,720
for that step, and then you look at the value of where

1073
00:54:06,720 --> 00:54:08,800
you end up, and the sum of those things together

1074
00:54:08,800 --> 00:54:11,520
tells you how good it was to be in your original state.

1075
00:54:11,520 --> 00:54:13,840
That's still true in an MDP.

1076
00:54:13,840 --> 00:54:16,240
Now what we're saying is that you start in this state,

1077
00:54:16,240 --> 00:54:18,400
and we know that we're following policy pi,

1078
00:54:18,400 --> 00:54:19,640
but the value thing in this state

1079
00:54:19,640 --> 00:54:22,160
is still the immediate reward that you get

1080
00:54:22,200 --> 00:54:25,120
plus the value of your successor state,

1081
00:54:25,120 --> 00:54:26,760
if you know that you're going to follow policy pi

1082
00:54:26,760 --> 00:54:28,520
from there onwards.

1083
00:54:28,520 --> 00:54:32,080
So we just look at what happened for one step

1084
00:54:32,080 --> 00:54:34,000
of following a policy, and then we ask,

1085
00:54:34,000 --> 00:54:37,040
how much more reward will I get following that policy?

1086
00:54:37,040 --> 00:54:38,160
And we sum those things together,

1087
00:54:38,160 --> 00:54:40,640
that gives us the Bellman equation.

1088
00:54:40,640 --> 00:54:42,600
We can do the same thing with the action value function,

1089
00:54:42,600 --> 00:54:45,880
with these Q values, and this basically tells us,

1090
00:54:45,880 --> 00:54:50,880
now, if I'm in one state, and I take an action from there,

1091
00:54:51,480 --> 00:54:53,800
then I'll get some immediate reward for that action,

1092
00:54:53,800 --> 00:54:56,360
for that specific action, and then I'll look at

1093
00:54:56,360 --> 00:54:58,080
where I end up, and I can ask, well,

1094
00:54:58,080 --> 00:55:01,600
what's the action value of the state that I end up in

1095
00:55:01,600 --> 00:55:06,000
under the action that I would pick from that point onwards?

1096
00:55:09,960 --> 00:55:12,880
So let's look at these kind of pictorially

1097
00:55:12,880 --> 00:55:14,000
and try and understand them with these

1098
00:55:14,000 --> 00:55:16,520
sort of look ahead search diagrams.

1099
00:55:16,520 --> 00:55:19,080
So the way to understand this is, first of all,

1100
00:55:19,080 --> 00:55:21,720
to understand how V and Q relate to each other.

1101
00:55:21,720 --> 00:55:23,640
So if you're in a state value function here,

1102
00:55:23,640 --> 00:55:27,160
really what it's saying is that we're gonna average over,

1103
00:55:27,160 --> 00:55:29,680
basically we're gonna average over the actions

1104
00:55:31,520 --> 00:55:33,360
that we might take.

1105
00:55:33,360 --> 00:55:36,240
So the sub-probability that we'll take this action here,

1106
00:55:36,240 --> 00:55:37,840
these black dots represent actions,

1107
00:55:37,840 --> 00:55:40,880
and these open circles represent states now.

1108
00:55:40,880 --> 00:55:42,920
So we're in some state here.

1109
00:55:42,920 --> 00:55:45,280
We might take this action here,

1110
00:55:45,280 --> 00:55:47,080
we might take this action here.

1111
00:55:47,080 --> 00:55:48,680
The probabilities of these things are defined

1112
00:55:48,800 --> 00:55:49,640
by our policy.

1113
00:55:49,640 --> 00:55:51,120
So this is like the probability I'll go left,

1114
00:55:51,120 --> 00:55:53,120
the probability I'll go right.

1115
00:55:53,120 --> 00:55:55,200
Over each of those actions we might take,

1116
00:55:55,200 --> 00:55:57,360
there's a Q value, there's an action value

1117
00:55:57,360 --> 00:55:59,120
telling us how good it is to take that action

1118
00:55:59,120 --> 00:56:00,640
from that state.

1119
00:56:00,640 --> 00:56:02,680
And so we're doing like a one step look ahead,

1120
00:56:02,680 --> 00:56:05,160
saying the state value, how good it is to do this state,

1121
00:56:05,160 --> 00:56:07,960
we just look ahead one step, look at the action values,

1122
00:56:07,960 --> 00:56:10,120
average them together, and that tells us the value

1123
00:56:10,120 --> 00:56:12,120
of being in that state at the top there.

1124
00:56:14,360 --> 00:56:17,880
Now let's do the converse, understand the opposite step.

1125
00:56:17,920 --> 00:56:21,160
So what happens if we start off taking some action?

1126
00:56:21,160 --> 00:56:24,760
So now the root of this tree is a state,

1127
00:56:24,760 --> 00:56:26,200
and we're considering a specific action

1128
00:56:26,200 --> 00:56:27,320
that we take from that state,

1129
00:56:27,320 --> 00:56:28,960
that's this black circle here.

1130
00:56:28,960 --> 00:56:33,320
So this is like saying I'm in this particular state here,

1131
00:56:33,320 --> 00:56:34,800
and I'm considering, I'm asking the question,

1132
00:56:34,800 --> 00:56:37,760
how good is it to go right from that state?

1133
00:56:38,920 --> 00:56:41,080
And how good it is, we now have to average

1134
00:56:41,080 --> 00:56:43,200
over the dynamics of our MDE,

1135
00:56:43,200 --> 00:56:45,920
the environment might blow me over here,

1136
00:56:46,760 --> 00:56:48,000
so after I've gone right,

1137
00:56:48,000 --> 00:56:50,880
I might get blown to all these different situations,

1138
00:56:50,880 --> 00:56:52,800
and we want to ask for each of these situations

1139
00:56:52,800 --> 00:56:54,640
I might get blown to, how good is it?

1140
00:56:54,640 --> 00:56:56,520
What's the value of being in that situation

1141
00:56:56,520 --> 00:56:59,920
following my policy after that point?

1142
00:56:59,920 --> 00:57:02,040
We average over all these things,

1143
00:57:03,040 --> 00:57:07,040
using the probabilities of our transition dynamics,

1144
00:57:07,400 --> 00:57:08,880
we average them together,

1145
00:57:08,880 --> 00:57:11,040
and that gives us the action value function

1146
00:57:11,040 --> 00:57:12,040
of the roots here.

1147
00:57:13,040 --> 00:57:13,880
Okay?

1148
00:57:16,240 --> 00:57:20,760
So V is telling us how good is it to be in a particular state,

1149
00:57:20,760 --> 00:57:22,000
Q is telling us how good is it

1150
00:57:22,000 --> 00:57:24,680
to take a particular action from a given state,

1151
00:57:24,680 --> 00:57:26,880
state value function, action value function.

1152
00:57:29,960 --> 00:57:31,240
We can put these together now,

1153
00:57:31,240 --> 00:57:33,560
so this is basically just stitching together

1154
00:57:33,560 --> 00:57:36,160
the two figures from the last two slides,

1155
00:57:36,160 --> 00:57:38,320
and now what we'll see is we get like a recursion

1156
00:57:38,320 --> 00:57:40,840
that helps us understand V in terms of itself,

1157
00:57:40,840 --> 00:57:42,880
and this is how we end up solving

1158
00:57:42,880 --> 00:57:44,280
Markov decision processes.

1159
00:57:45,240 --> 00:57:47,120
So at the roots of the tree now,

1160
00:57:47,120 --> 00:57:49,800
we've got the value function for a particular state,

1161
00:57:49,800 --> 00:57:51,480
tells us how good is it to be in this state,

1162
00:57:51,480 --> 00:57:52,920
and the way we're gonna understand that

1163
00:57:52,920 --> 00:57:55,600
is by doing a two-step look ahead.

1164
00:57:55,600 --> 00:57:57,120
So if we look ahead, we could consider

1165
00:57:57,120 --> 00:57:59,080
all the actions we might take next,

1166
00:57:59,080 --> 00:58:00,760
go left, go right,

1167
00:58:00,760 --> 00:58:01,800
we could consider all the things

1168
00:58:01,800 --> 00:58:03,560
that the environment might do to us,

1169
00:58:03,560 --> 00:58:06,320
might blow me over, it might keep me standing up,

1170
00:58:06,320 --> 00:58:08,840
might blow me over, it might keep me standing up,

1171
00:58:08,840 --> 00:58:10,640
there are all the things the environment might do,

1172
00:58:10,640 --> 00:58:12,240
and then for each of those things the environment might do,

1173
00:58:12,240 --> 00:58:14,600
there's some successor state that we'll end up in,

1174
00:58:14,600 --> 00:58:17,360
we want to know well how good is it to be in that state

1175
00:58:17,360 --> 00:58:19,400
and carry on with my usual policy,

1176
00:58:19,400 --> 00:58:22,720
how much reward will I get if I carry on from that point.

1177
00:58:22,720 --> 00:58:25,200
Now if we average these things all together,

1178
00:58:25,200 --> 00:58:26,600
we're basically averaging in two ways,

1179
00:58:26,600 --> 00:58:28,440
now we're averaging over our policy,

1180
00:58:29,360 --> 00:58:31,960
we're weighting each of these arcs by the probability

1181
00:58:31,960 --> 00:58:34,440
that a policy will select left or right,

1182
00:58:34,440 --> 00:58:36,320
we're averaging each of these arcs

1183
00:58:36,320 --> 00:58:38,960
by the transition probabilities

1184
00:58:38,960 --> 00:58:41,520
that will end up getting blown in one direction or another,

1185
00:58:41,520 --> 00:58:42,920
we average it all together,

1186
00:58:42,920 --> 00:58:44,000
and that gives us the value of being

1187
00:58:44,000 --> 00:58:45,840
in the root of this diagram here,

1188
00:58:45,840 --> 00:58:48,760
tells us how good is it to be in a particular state.

1189
00:58:48,760 --> 00:58:50,360
So this is the Bellman equation.

1190
00:58:52,160 --> 00:58:54,440
You can do exactly the same thing for action values,

1191
00:58:54,440 --> 00:58:56,040
there's a recursive relationship,

1192
00:58:56,040 --> 00:58:57,000
you can look at this offline,

1193
00:58:57,000 --> 00:58:58,800
I don't want to sort of belabor the point,

1194
00:58:58,800 --> 00:59:01,000
but it's just stitching the diagram together

1195
00:59:01,000 --> 00:59:02,160
the other way around,

1196
00:59:02,160 --> 00:59:04,200
and we end up with exactly the same idea

1197
00:59:04,200 --> 00:59:06,640
that starting from a particular state in action,

1198
00:59:06,640 --> 00:59:08,320
we can now look ahead two steps,

1199
00:59:08,320 --> 00:59:10,960
consider where the wind might blow us first now,

1200
00:59:10,960 --> 00:59:12,720
and then consider from the state

1201
00:59:12,720 --> 00:59:14,560
the wind might blow us to,

1202
00:59:14,560 --> 00:59:16,000
which action might I take next?

1203
00:59:16,000 --> 00:59:18,480
Am I choose to go left or am I choose to go right?

1204
00:59:18,480 --> 00:59:20,720
And now we average over this thing again,

1205
00:59:20,720 --> 00:59:23,000
we look at the value of that particular action,

1206
00:59:23,000 --> 00:59:25,560
the Q value, average these things all together,

1207
00:59:25,560 --> 00:59:26,920
and it tells us something about the Q value

1208
00:59:26,920 --> 00:59:29,160
of this start action here.

1209
00:59:29,160 --> 00:59:32,840
So the Q value relates to the Q values of the next step,

1210
00:59:32,840 --> 00:59:34,840
in this slide, and the previous slide,

